WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.310
♪ Super Sonic ♪

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.940
- Good morning, my rural chum!

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.550
- Mister?
- Doctor Robotnik.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.440
I'm going to give you five seconds

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.980
to tell me where it is.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.098
- Wait, don't hurt him!

00:00:11.098 --> 00:00:11.931
(screaming)

00:00:11.931 --> 00:00:12.770
- So first of all,

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.200
earlier on this year at CinemaCon,

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.480
you said that you liked,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.770
you really liked playing
Robotnik for a couple of reasons.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.990
You said you got to explore
a new side of yourself

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.230
and hit the absurd energy people love.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.080
- Yeah.
- So I'm wondering,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.950
what did you find out about
yourself playing this role?

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.139
- I'm not that smart.

00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:28.705
(Nikki laughing)

00:00:28.705 --> 00:00:29.538
- Anybody by comparison.
- I'm not that great

00:00:29.538 --> 00:00:30.595
at video games.

00:00:30.595 --> 00:00:32.100
(laughing)

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.150
But the wonderful thing about it is,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.540
I can be really lousy at this video game

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.950
and still win because I'm Robotnik.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.480
- You are the most--
- So literally.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.910
- Evil person in the world.
- When I see

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.700
the big machine Robotnik coming by,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.200
I just ram Sonic into him.
- Yes.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.835
- Over and over again until he goes.

00:00:48.835 --> 00:00:50.100
(laughing)

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.740
Flies off the game grid.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.560
- He's such an iconic character.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.370
- Yeah.
- Beloved by the Sonic OGs.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:56.460
- Yeah.
- And you've played

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.590
so many iconic characters.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.070
So how did you approach Robotnik?

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.610
- Basically, I,

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:03.710
I believe that light,

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.190
you and I don't have to believe it,

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.610
it's true scientifically.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.590
Lightning starts from the ground.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:10.730
So that's the same with the character,

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.900
with this kinda character,

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.960
is like I had to find that

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.770
evil genius,

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:20.000
the combination of absolute intelligence

00:01:21.595 --> 00:01:24.790
and total worthlessness in his own mind.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.510
That combination is deadly

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.275
and we're seeing a lot of it.

00:01:28.275 --> 00:01:29.274
We're seeing a lot of it.
- That also sounds

00:01:29.274 --> 00:01:32.079
like Hollywood.
- It sounds like billionaires.

00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:32.912
- [Nikki] Yes.

00:01:32.912 --> 00:01:33.745
- It's not Hollywood.

00:01:33.745 --> 00:01:36.000
It's everywhere, it's everywhere.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.070
So that's a wonderful combination to play

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.884
plus the idea of AI
freaking everybody out.

00:01:41.884 --> 00:01:42.717
- [Nikki] Yeah.

00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:44.820
- They're putting chips in people's heads

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.500
and Alexa is spying on you

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.327
and watching you and saying,

00:01:48.327 --> 00:01:50.910
"You might wanna change your underwear."

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.200
- I know in the film,

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.380
to sort of display Sonic's speed.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.520
- Yeah.
- They have everything else

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.950
freeze around him 'cause he's so quick.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.360
- Right.
- Your humor is so quick.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.840
When you are in a scene

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.400
is everything frozen around you?

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.240
What do you experience

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.650
when you're going at lightning speed?

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.074
- Everybody's dumbfounded.
- Everybody's, "Err."

00:02:08.074 --> 00:02:09.660
- Well generally,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.100
generally it goes,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.200
the world spins for me.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.483
It's wild.

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:19.060
My world is a game grid.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.990
And I look at it like
every little segment,

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:23.930
every little moment,

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.560
like this moment with you

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.680
is the only thing that is, right?

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.010
And then there's something else

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.450
and then the next moment
is the only thing that is.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.150
And I try to completely dedicate
myself to those moments.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.600
And bring whatever I can to those moments.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.330
And in the same way when I perform,

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.360
I'm in my hotel room,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.570
I'm walking in a circle,

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.830
I'm saying the lines to
myself, and I learn them.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.440
And then my brain starts creating

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.650
and screws up all my learning

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.090
and the next thing you
know, I've dreamt about it,

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.660
I'm walking, over breakfast.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.320
I have some ideas, and then
I come in with 20 new ideas,

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.240
a third of which I can remember.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.170
The rest are on little
cards tied up everywhere.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.640
And--
- Your hotel rooms

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.017
must look really cool.
- It's like,

00:03:08.017 --> 00:03:09.860
(laser beam zapping)
(laughing)

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.370
Whatever's in my consciousness.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.260
Whatever gets my attention
becomes part of the show.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.210
- So Sonic has a bucket
list in this movie.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.400
Are you, because you do
what you're just saying,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.233
just living in the moment.

00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:20.066
Do you have a bucket list?

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:21.433
Are there things that you really wanna do?

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:23.190
- I wanna explode into a ball of light.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.120
- Do you?
- Yes.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.280
- Yeah.
- That's big.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.130
- Yeah.
- That's a big one.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.061
- Number one.
- Yes.

00:03:27.061 --> 00:03:27.894
- Then there's nothing else

00:03:27.894 --> 00:03:28.727
comes after that?
- Yeah.

00:03:28.727 --> 00:03:31.170
I wanna awaken spiritually
over and over again.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.248
And then go back to sleep.

00:03:32.248 --> 00:03:33.600
(laughing)

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.420
And hit the snooze alarm.
- Right.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.810
Is there something you've accomplished,

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.390
something bucket list-ish
that you have done?

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:46.390
- I am literally black
and blue with pinch marks,

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:48.660
from my life.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:52.040
It is a constant surprise.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.080
A constant

00:03:55.489 --> 00:03:57.680
just a gratitude soup.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:58.513
Honest to God.

00:03:58.513 --> 00:04:00.217
Every bite of it, I just go,

00:04:00.217 --> 00:04:01.417
"I can't believe I'm here.

00:04:01.417 --> 00:04:03.767
"I can't believe I get to do what I do."

00:04:05.051 --> 00:04:06.720
I love my audience.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.050
I love what I do.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.160
And I get to do not only that,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.090
I get to produce a TV show and star on it.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.010
I get to

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.890
write a novel/memoir
that's coming out in May.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:20.793
I get to,

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.190
become a cartoonist and an artist

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.450
and actually be taken seriously that way

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:30.093
and contribute in New York
and Playboy Magazines.

00:04:31.814 --> 00:04:35.380
It is an embarrassment of
riches and opportunities

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.230
and prosperity and abundance.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:39.170
And I honestly,

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.820
I feel like I'm,

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.140
and I say this all the time,

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.770
I talk about mangoes.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.820
I look at it like life

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:47.830
has just become a gigantic mango tree.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.000
And I'm sitting underneath it

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.950
and they're just falling all around me,

00:04:51.896 --> 00:04:53.670
and it's wonderful.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.130
- You made a joke about your haircut,

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.629
how it took seven haircuts to grow out.

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:58.260
- Yes.
- So I wanted to say

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:59.180
from women everywhere

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.619
who have had bad haircuts.

00:05:00.619 --> 00:05:01.910
(laughing)

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.460
We fully appreciate

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.400
what you've gone through.
_ Yeah.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:05.790
Well they cut that,

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:07.890
that part into my hair.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.250
It was a nice big thick swath

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.130
and it screwed everything up.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.370
They wanted it to be seen
from outer space or something.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.480
I don't know.
- So you understand

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.820
what women go through.
- Oh, yes.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.420
I can't say I totally understand

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:21.220
but yes.
- Thank you.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.780
- I empathize.
- Yes.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:24.300
- Oh my god, stop the car right now!

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.990
- What? What?
- The world's largest

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:27.150
rubber band ball?

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:28.040
We gotta see it.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.000
- No, this is not some
fun family road trip!

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.770
- Eh, you're right.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:32.603
It was lame.

00:05:32.603 --> 00:05:33.644
Gift shop was cool though.

00:05:33.644 --> 00:05:35.410
(thudding)

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:36.660
- This is a movie reference.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.390
People have to see the
movie to understand this.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:39.500
Although it is in the trailer.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:40.333
- It is.
- A little bit.

00:05:40.333 --> 00:05:41.470
- If you go to Fandango's
Twitter, it's there.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:42.460
- There.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:44.286
You're on our Twitter too
'cause you retweeted us.

00:05:44.286 --> 00:05:45.627
Thank you.
- I know, I was very excited.

00:05:45.627 --> 00:05:47.360
You posted a clip today

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.667
that although I don't
know when it's coming out.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:49.700
But you posted a clip.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:50.533
How do you do it?

00:05:50.533 --> 00:05:51.620
You do it this way?
- I do it that way

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:52.466
but I'm Canadian.

00:05:52.466 --> 00:05:53.390
(laughing)

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:54.670
- So you do it a totally different way?

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:55.570
- I don't know.
- You posted

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.230
a clip today that, oh.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:00.270
Oh, you posted.
- You're very good at it.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.310
- Yeah.
- I actually shortened

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:02.817
your string so you'd be better than me.

00:06:02.817 --> 00:06:03.820
(laughing)

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:05.480
- You posted a clip today
and I was so excited you did

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.590
because it's this speech
but there's a line in it

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.620
that I tried to copy
Jim Carrey's performance

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:10.780
in "Dumb and Dumber."
- Stop it.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.910
- So there's a line that he
does in "Dumb and Dumber"

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:13.950
where he's like,

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:14.930
he's talking about this thing,

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:18.890
and then he goes, "Our pets'
heads are falling off!"

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.480
- We got no food!

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.090
We got no jobs!

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.860
Our pets' heads are fallin' off!

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.210
- And I did that for this.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.430
I go, "I got a fish on my head!"

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.700
- I'm wet.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.090
I'm cold.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.750
There's a fish on my head!

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.600
- We had fans pick their
most iconic Sonic lines.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:35.790
The OG fans.
- Are you kidding me?

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.050
You need glasses.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:38.320
You went like this to read that?

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.310
- No, I want you to do it.
- Oh.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.143
Okay, so you want--
- I don't wanna do the voice.

00:06:41.143 --> 00:06:43.401
- You want me to do Sonic?
- Yes.

00:06:43.401 --> 00:06:44.530
- Okay.

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:45.363
Okay.

00:06:45.363 --> 00:06:46.750
You might know everything I'm gonna do

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:47.950
but that's not gonna help you.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:49.870
Since I know everything you're gonna do.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.238
Strange isn't it.

00:06:51.238 --> 00:06:52.071
(laughing)

00:06:52.071 --> 00:06:53.290
I found you, faker!

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.300
I'll make you eat those words!

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.000
You're too slow!

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:57.840
And what's this one?

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.210
Fandango's great.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.850
That's not from a cartoon show.

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:02.460
- You are the master improver

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.680
'cause that was not on the,

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:04.721
that was not on the card.
- Oh wow.

00:07:04.721 --> 00:07:05.670
Oh my goodness.
- Wow.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:06.920
That just, that was incredible

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:07.753
watching that.
- Want me to keep this?

00:07:07.753 --> 00:07:08.620
- You can keep it.
- Oh really?

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.030
- And the paddle board.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.140
- Okay.
- It's incredible watching it

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:12.260
because it's hard to do.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.500
It's really hard to do

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.990
but you've done a couple of voices before.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.247
So how did you change it up for this one?

00:07:17.247 --> 00:07:19.070
And did you pay homage to the originals?

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:19.903
How did you?

00:07:19.903 --> 00:07:20.736
- Sure.
- Yeah.

00:07:20.736 --> 00:07:22.010
- So there've been
incredible voiceover actors

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:22.843
that have done this voice.

00:07:22.843 --> 00:07:24.080
- Yeah.
- There's Roger Craig,

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:25.450
there's Jaleel, there's
Ryan, there's Jason.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:27.030
There's a long list of incredible people.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.390
And people who've done Robotnik, man.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:30.460
There's some great
Robotnik voices out there.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.900
But the way that I did it was

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:33.610
I played the video games one more time

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.620
and then I read the script and in my head,

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:36.830
the way that the script played was like,

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.850
it like this young kid
who has so much energy,

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:41.480
who's never been allowed out of this cave,

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:42.530
finally gets out of this cave,

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:44.820
gets to do whatever he
does and he's so excited.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.870
So I kind of just played
it like a little kid

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.700
that's finally so excited to
do whatever he wanted to do.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:51.350
And it just happens to look like that.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.269
So when you're watching the movie,

00:07:52.269 --> 00:07:53.700
you feel the warmth and connection

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:54.990
between Tom and this character

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.750
'cause it feels like a real little boy.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:57.583
You know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:07:57.583 --> 00:07:58.580
- That's kinda where I was coming from.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:01.130
- Wow, I love that.
- Oh, great.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:01.963
- And playing the video game.

00:08:01.963 --> 00:08:03.050
That wasn't really research, was it?

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:04.222
You just wanted to.
- Oh no.

00:08:04.222 --> 00:08:06.240
I remember literally when I got the role,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:07.617
I was like, "Oh, I can write off

00:08:07.617 --> 00:08:09.757
"that I've already bought
Sonic Mania, I guess.

00:08:09.757 --> 00:08:11.400
"Now it's a business expense?"

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:12.810
Something like that.
- 'Cause there's a lot of talk

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:13.940
about bucket list in this movie.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.480
So did you check off a bucket list item

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:17.760
playing Sonic?
- For many things.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.850
One, which is to be Sonic,

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:21.690
a character that I've loved growing up.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:24.690
The other one, to be in a
movie that stars Jim Carrey.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:25.710
That's something that--
- Yeah.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:26.650
- If you asked me as a kid,

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:27.830
I couldn't even dream that big.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.300
I never thought I'd be doing any of this.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:30.410
I was a psych-anthro major.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.000
So the idea that any of this is happening

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:33.920
is above and beyond.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:35.070
I feel very very lucky.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.109
- So I know you're a gamer.

00:08:36.109 --> 00:08:37.550
You're a huge gamer.
- I am a gamer.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:39.120
- So for the OG Sonic fans,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.373
what are they gonna find in this movie

00:08:40.373 --> 00:08:41.206
that they're gonna love?

00:08:41.206 --> 00:08:42.660
- I mean I could tell
you specific Easter eggs,

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:43.620
if you want.
- Go for it.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.500
- Okay, there's some
Easter eggs in the game.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:47.600
There's one that if you're
Sonic and you go near ledge,

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:48.660
he goes like this.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.390
In the movie, in the
hotel scene there's that.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:51.600
If you look at his bandana,

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.190
when he playing with his nunchucks,

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.260
the old emblem of the
start screen is there,

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.463
and there's a bunch of different ones

00:08:56.463 --> 00:08:58.170
that I won't talk about now.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.570
But I think what,

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:00.760
the cool thing that Jeff and all,

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:01.940
everybody did and the writers did,

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.790
is that they reward the fans

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:04.623
that have been there for a while

00:09:04.623 --> 00:09:05.630
with all these fun Easter eggs.

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.090
And they help introduce Sonic

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:09.430
in a way that if you've
never seen this before,

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:10.700
you still get to love it

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:13.410
'cause it's got action, comedy,
drama, heart, Jim Carrey.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.300
And it comes together in
this fun beautiful way.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.443
- Clearly, I'm not gonna be
able to do this on my own.

00:09:21.203 --> 00:09:22.036
(fish sloshing)

00:09:22.036 --> 00:09:24.255
(thudding)

00:09:24.255 --> 00:09:25.180
(sighing)

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:26.013
- All right.

00:09:26.013 --> 00:09:26.896
Get in the truck.
- Really?

00:09:26.896 --> 00:09:28.730
You're gonna help me?

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:30.070
- I guess it is a little bit my fault

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.160
that all this is happening to you.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:32.070
- Not a little bit.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:32.940
Entirely!

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.860
It is entirely your fault.
- Okay.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:35.693
It's entirely my fault.

00:09:35.693 --> 00:09:36.750
Are you coming?

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:37.583
- Yes.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.370
(door thudding)

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:41.873
Road trip, whoop whoop!

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:43.630
- What am I doing?

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:45.440
- [Nikki] So this opens
on Valentine's Day.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.100
- That's right.
- It's such a

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.940
counterintuitive to Valentine's Day

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:51.963
but why is this the perfect
Valentine's weekend movie?

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:53.793
- Why is it?

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:57.390
Because I think that at the
core of every good relationship

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:58.423
is a friendship.

00:09:59.258 --> 00:10:00.091
(snorting)

00:10:00.091 --> 00:10:01.162
- That was brilliant!

00:10:01.162 --> 00:10:03.000
(laughing)

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.468
- I actually do believe that.

00:10:04.468 --> 00:10:05.520
- Yeah.
- But yeah.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.690
Maybe for all the people
who don't wanna do this

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:09.440
sort of straight down the line,

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.410
what you're supposed to
do on Valentine's Day.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.279
Go see a fun movie that you
can both laugh at together.

00:10:15.279 --> 00:10:16.350
Laugh at.
- This movie is so,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:17.720
laugh with.
- Yes.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:18.553
- Laugh alone.

00:10:18.553 --> 00:10:19.777
Cry alone on Valentine's.
- Yes, get a laugh at it.

00:10:19.777 --> 00:10:20.610
- Whatever.
- Laugh at it.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.330
Laugh all you want, laugh it.

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:23.670
Every preposition you can think of.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.860
- So you are so phenomenal in this movie.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:26.787
- Oh, thank you, thank you.

00:10:26.787 --> 00:10:28.440
- And what I always do,

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.610
whenever I watch a movie
that has CGI characters,

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:31.660
I'm looking for the cracks.

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:33.250
I'm looking for when
the actor, I can tell.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:34.208
- Same.
- There's no CGI.

00:10:34.208 --> 00:10:35.430
Do you do the same thing?
- I do the same thing, yes.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:36.780
- I couldn't see any with you.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:37.820
- That was my goal.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:38.930
I was like, I want to make them

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:39.880
feel like this is real,

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:41.967
and sometimes the camera
operators will be like,

00:10:41.967 --> 00:10:43.210
"Ah, just look over here."

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:44.043
I'm like, "No no no.

00:10:44.043 --> 00:10:46.220
"I wanna know exactly where his eyes are."

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:47.053
'Cause you can tell

00:10:47.053 --> 00:10:49.680
if you're looking 15 feet in the distance

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:50.770
or two feet, right?
- Yeah.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:52.090
- Your eyes triangulate.

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:53.190
Anyway, well good.

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:54.360
I'm glad you feel the same way.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.193
Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:10:55.193 --> 00:10:56.520
So just also for you,

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:57.940
when you're on the day,

00:10:57.940 --> 00:10:59.350
did you have voices?

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:00.350
Did they play voices?

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:01.668
Did you play against the sound?

00:11:01.668 --> 00:11:03.210
- Yes, there was a gentleman there

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.650
who was a really talented and funny guy

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.180
who was reading opposite me.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.840
But he was hidden that
I couldn't hear him.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:11.833
He knew,

00:11:12.689 --> 00:11:15.480
he got the sense of Ben's sensibilities

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.000
and his comic timing,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.133
and he was a great help
when Ben wasn't there,

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:23.180
so that there was someone
reading the lines with you

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:26.580
and created the rhythm of the scene.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.840
But as far as something to
look at, it was nothing.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:29.673
There was no,

00:11:29.673 --> 00:11:32.826
I tried to get Ben in a full
blue spandex suit there.

00:11:32.826 --> 00:11:33.780
(laughing)

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.320
- That would have been great.
- But he was busy

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:37.890
and too tall, it turns out.
- Right.

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:40.557
That would've been great
for the extras on the DVD.

00:11:40.557 --> 00:11:41.538
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:11:41.538 --> 00:11:43.190
Maybe, maybe there will be.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:44.720
- So I know you're a bit of a gamer

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:45.960
or at least you were a bit of a gamer.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:49.810
What do you think the OG fans of Sonic

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:52.440
are gonna appreciate about this movie?

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.700
- I think they're gonna love,

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:57.110
'cause look, there's a certain,

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.010
there's a certain mythology with the game

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.767
and certain lore

00:12:00.767 --> 00:12:04.243
that we expanded upon to
make it a film, right?

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:08.170
So the storyline, some
of those things weren't,

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.280
Sonic never spoke in
the video games, right?

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.770
And suddenly now Ben
Schwartz is voicing him.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:14.810
But I think the fans who
grew up playing the game

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:18.970
are gonna love seeing the
character come to life.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:20.240
He now has a voice.

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:21.410
He now has a personality.

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:24.040
He's cocky, he sorta, he sarcastic

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.243
but there's an innocence, a purity to him.

00:12:27.449 --> 00:12:29.780
And it's like,

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:32.470
anybody who loved something growing up

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:34.500
that existed in a two-dimensional world,

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.480
seeing it step into the 3rd
dimension and come to life.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:38.610
- Something about your childhood.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:39.580
When you were--
- Yeah, hearing the ching

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:41.090
of when he gets the rings.
- Yes.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:43.137
- All those nostalgic

00:12:43.137 --> 00:12:45.230
markers that just make you happy

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:46.450
and that bring you back to your childhood.

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:47.980
- For you, what were those games?

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:48.930
Was it Sonic?

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:49.810
And what are the other ones

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:51.820
that maybe would make a great movie?

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:53.050
- Yeah, that's a good question.

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:55.870
I was like 18 or 19 when Sonic came out.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:56.980
I was still video gaming.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:58.840
But I started with Atari

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:03.077
and computer games like
an old Commodore 64

00:13:03.077 --> 00:13:06.250
and the television and
Nintendo and Super Nintendo.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:08.460
I spent, the games I
spent the most time on

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:10.653
were probably the original Mario Brothers,

00:13:11.986 --> 00:13:12.819
Sonic,

00:13:14.737 --> 00:13:16.760
and GoldenEye.

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:17.593
- Love it!

00:13:17.593 --> 00:13:18.426
- Yeah, GoldenEye.

00:13:18.426 --> 00:13:21.910
That was the first first-person
shooter kinda thing

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:24.410
and I spent way too
many hours playing that.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:25.243
I had to quit.

00:13:25.243 --> 00:13:26.290
I had to quit all video games.

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:27.123
- Did you really?
- Yeah.

00:13:27.123 --> 00:13:28.220
'Cause I just would get sucked,

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:30.560
I would just not get anything done

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:31.970
and I would just get sucked in

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.770
and four hours would pass like that.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:39.260
So I have a great place in
my heart for video gamers,

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:40.570
for video games period.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:42.620
Sonic is one of the
great iconic characters.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:44.972
So it's a special thing to
bring him to the big screen.

00:13:44.972 --> 00:13:47.300
(upbeat rock music)

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:49.420
- [Robotnik] Whatever this creature is

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:51.910
I'm going to uncover the
source of its powers.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:52.743
Yeah, hey!

00:13:52.743 --> 00:13:55.691
(upbeat rock music)

